Subject: A plain object is allocated without the generic allocator's work, and a constructor's common create has no slow path inlined

What this does
  - js_new_plain_object: what JS_NewObjectFromShapeCapExtra does for a JS_CLASS_OBJECT object with no properties handed in. It applies the same capacity rule, clears the flags as the two bytes before class_id in one go (checked by a static_assert), then fills the header and the GC list. There is no class switch. JS_NewObjectFromShapeCap takes it for that case.
  - js_create_from_ctor_cap is split. Its common case (a bytecode constructor whose shape still has the cached `prototype` slot, holding an object it keeps an instance shape for) is a small function that allocates through js_new_plain_object. Everything else (a fact event to report, a layout to build, any other constructor or class) runs the unchanged body, now js_create_from_ctor_cap_slow and out of line, so its property read no longer weighs on the common path.

Why we need it
  Every `new C()` of a plain constructor paid about 160 instructions in these two functions. raytrace C.Minstr 1002.3 -> 966.1. The plain interpreter (I.Minstr) 1982.9 -> 1947.7: this is the engine's general construct path.

Soundness
  The same checks in the same order and the same object, field for field. Only the code layout changes.

--- a/quickjs.c
